class Solution {
  public int switchboardRingsFirstQualifyingSlip(int[][] links, int k) {
    int[][] a=links; List<Integer> out=new ArrayList<>();
    {int n=a.length;int[]dist=new int[n];Arrays.fill(dist,-1);Deque<Integer>queue=new ArrayDeque<>();if(n>0){dist[0]=0;queue.add(0);}while(!queue.isEmpty()){int u=queue.remove();for(int v=0;v<n;v++)if(a[u][v]!=0&&dist[v]==-1){dist[v]=dist[u]+1;queue.add(v);}}for(int d:dist)out.add(d);}
    for(int x:out)if(x>=k)return x;return -1;
  }
}

privateData.editorialMd:
Build the result list

A graph is a set of points joined by paths. Here, paths go in one direction. Start at point 0. For each point, find the fewest paths you must follow to reach it. Record these distances in point order: 0, 1, 2, and so on. The distance to point 0 is 0. Record -1 for a point you cannot reach. A graph with no points gives an empty result list.

Return the answer

Read the result list from left to right. Return the first number that is at least k (greater than or equal to k). Return the number, not its position. If none matches, return -1.

Why this works

Each value in the result list follows Step 1. Step 2 uses only those values. Keeping these steps separate helps avoid returning the list when the question asks for one number. Check empty input, one item, repeated values, and the largest allowed input.

Efficiency goal: time O(V^2); extra memory, including the result list, O(n). n is the input length; V is the number of graph points. These labels describe how work and memory grow with the input.

